==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|naught|y}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈnɔːti/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈnɔti/|/ˈnɑti/|lang=en}}
* {{homophones|lang=en|knotty}} {{qualifier|in accents with the [[w:cot-caught merger|cot-caught merger]]}}
* {{audio|en-us-naughty.ogg|Audio (US cot-caught merged)}}
* {{rhymes|ɔːti}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# {{context|now|_|rare|archaic|lang=en}} [[evil|Evil]], [[wicked]], morally [[reprehensible]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* '''1623''', {{w|William Shakespeare}}, First Folio, ''The Merchant of Venice'':
#*: So shines a good deed in a '''naughty''' world.
#* '''1644''', {{w|John Milton}}, ''Aeropagitica'':
#*: Wholesome meats to a vitiated stomack differ little or nothing from unwholesome; and best books to a '''naughty''' mind are not unappliable to occasions of evill.
#* {{rfdate}} Udall:
#*: Such as be intemperant, that is, followers of their '''naughty''' appetites and lusts.
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} [[bad|Bad]], [[worthless]], [[substandard]]. {{defdate|16th-19th c.}}
#* {{rfdate}} American King James Bible, Jeremiah 24:2:
#*: One basket had very good figs, even like the figs that are first ripe: and the other basket had very '''naughty''' figs, which could not be eaten, they were so bad.
# [[mischievous|Mischievous]]; tending to [[misbehave]] or act badly (especially of a child). {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#: ''Some '''naughty''' boys at school hid the teacher's lesson notes.''
# [[immoral|Immoral]], sexually provocative; now in weakened sense, [[risqué]], [[cheeky]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#: ''I bought some '''naughty''' lingerie for my honeymoon.''
#: ''If I see you send another '''naughty''' email to your friends, you will be forbidden from using the computer!''

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|immoral, sexually provocative}} [[dirty]]
* {{sense|mischievous}} [[mischievous]]

====Antonyms====
